              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Ask for one or two corrections</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Identify useful improvements</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">18–20 min</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Review new words and say goodbye</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Turn the conversation into a learning record</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>This is only an example structure. Beginners may need shorter sessions, while advanced learners may prefer longer discussions. The important part is having a purpose rather than treating every conversation as unstructured practice.</p>

      <h3>Learn Everyday Phrases</h3>
      <p>Before chatting, memorize 10–15 useful phrases you can use often. For example: greetings, how to ask questions politely, or how to express that you’re still learning (“I’m practicing my English — please speak slowly!”). These small tools make conversations flow better.</p>

      <h3>Choose Topics You Enjoy</h3>
      <p>Think about hobbies, travel, or music. When you talk about something you love, it’s easier to express yourself. On the website, you can start conversations around shared interests, making practice sessions feel natural and fun instead of formal.</p>

      <h3>Use a Notebook or Notes App</h3>
      <p>Keep a digital notebook or journal. After each chat, write down new words or phrases you learned. Reflect on them later and use them in your next session. Progress in language learning comes from consistent repetition.</p>

      <h2>4. How to Communicate Effectively with Native Speakers</h2>
      <p>Talking to someone in your second language can feel intimidating — especially the first few times. The key is to focus on communication, not perfection.</p>

      <div class="blog-research-box" style="width:100%;max-width:900px;margin:24px auto;padding:20px;box-sizing:border-box;border:1px solid #5a76a3;border-radius:12px;background:#263245b3;">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0;">Video, Voice, or Text: Which Is Better for Language Practice?</h3>

        <p>Different communication formats can create different learning experiences. Research comparing real-time video calls and text chat has found that both can produce opportunities for negotiating meaning, but the interaction patterns are not identical.</p>

        <p style="margin-bottom:0;">Video communication can provide additional visual information such as facial expressions and gestures, while text gives learners more time to process what they want to say. A useful approach is to choose the format that matches your current confidence and gradually increase speaking difficulty.</p>
      </div>

      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;opacity:0.8;"><strong>Research source:</strong> <em>Video call or chat? Negotiation of meaning and issues of face in telecollaboration</em>, System, 2014.</p>

      <h3>How to Make Corrections More Useful</h3>

      <div class="table-scroll" style="width:100%;max-width:100%;overflow-x:auto;-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;margin:24px 0;">
        <table class="blog-table" style="width:100%;min-width:700px;border-collapse:collapse;">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;text-align:left;">Less useful approach</th>
              <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;text-align:left;">Better approach</th>
              <th style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;text-align:left;">Why</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>

          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Correct every sentence</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Choose one or two important corrections</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Keeps conversation flowing</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Interrupt immediately</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Wait until the speaker finishes when the mistake doesn't prevent understanding</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Preserves confidence and communication</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Only point out mistakes</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Mention what was communicated successfully too</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Makes feedback more balanced</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Assume every correction is necessary</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Ask what kind of feedback the learner wants</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Different learners have different goals</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h3>Be Clear and Simple</h3>
      <p>Don’t try to use advanced words just to impress your partner. Simple, correct sentences are far more effective. Native speakers appreciate effort, not vocabulary size.</p>

      <h3>Ask Questions</h3>
      <p>Curiosity keeps the conversation alive. Ask about their favorite foods, local customs, or how certain words are used. This not only helps your vocabulary but also gives cultural context — which makes learning memorable.</p>

      <h3>Use Gestures and Expressions</h3>
      <p>If you forget a word, use gestures or describe what you mean. Communication is more than language. A smile and an expressive face go a long way in keeping the mood light and friendly.</p>

      <h3>Useful Phrases When You Get Stuck</h3>

      <p>You do not need to know every word to keep a conversation going. Learning a few communication-repair phrases can help you recover when you forget vocabulary or do not understand the other speaker.</p>

      <div class="table-scroll" style="width:100%;max-width:100%;overflow-x:auto;-webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch;margin:24px 0;">
        <table class="blog-table" style="width:100%;min-width:680px;border-collapse:collapse;">
          <thead>
