            <tr>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">You didn't hear clearly</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">"Could you say that again, please?"</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Request repetition</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">The speaker is too fast</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">"Could you speak a little more slowly?"</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Adjust speaking speed</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">You don't know a word</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">"What does ___ mean?"</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Ask for vocabulary help</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">You want to confirm meaning</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">"Do you mean ___?"</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Check your understanding</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">You need time to think</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">"Let me think for a second."</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Buy time without abandoning the conversation</td>
            </tr>

            <tr>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">You want correction</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">"Could you correct me if I say something incorrectly?"</td>
              <td style="padding:12px;border:1px solid #5a76a3;">Invite useful feedback</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>5. Turning Conversations into Learning Opportunities</h2>
      <p>Every video chat session is a learning opportunity if you reflect on it afterward. Don’t just talk and forget — learn from every moment. If you want more details about <a href="/blog/how-random-video-chatting-can-improve-your-english">random chatting apps</a>, read this guide.</p>

      <h3>Record New Words (With Permission)</h3>
      <p>If your partner agrees, record the audio or note new words during the chat. Later, replay and repeat phrases to improve pronunciation and recall. Hearing your own progress builds motivation.</p>

      <h3>Review After Each Chat</h3>
      <p>After every session, write down:<br />
      1️⃣ New words or idioms you heard.<br />
      2️⃣ Common mistakes you made.<br />
      3️⃣ Topics that were difficult to discuss.<br />
      This self-feedback loop turns casual chats into structured learning sessions.</p>

      <h3>Ask for Feedback</h3>
      <p>Most native speakers love helping learners. Politely ask, “Can you correct me if I say something wrong?” It shows humility and eagerness to learn. You’ll be surprised how many people enjoy helping others improve.</p>

      <h2>6. Building Confidence Over Time</h2>
      <p>Confidence doesn’t appear overnight — it grows with consistent effort. The more often you chat, the more natural you’ll sound. Regular exposure helps your brain form automatic patterns.</p>

      <div class="blog-research-box" style="width:100%;max-width:900px;margin:24px auto;padding:20px;box-sizing:border-box;border:1px solid #5a76a3;border-radius:12px;background:#263245b3;">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0;">Why Low-Pressure Speaking Practice Can Help</h3>

        <p>Speaking anxiety is a recognized challenge for people learning a foreign language. Research has examined whether online speaking activities can make practice feel less stressful.</p>

        <p style="margin-bottom:0;">One study of 70 university students learning English and Arabic as foreign languages found that students who participated in online speaking tasks reported positive attitudes toward the experience, and the study found statistically significant differences in speaking anxiety between the online-speaking and comparison groups.</p>
      </div>

      <p>The practical lesson is not that online conversation automatically removes anxiety. Instead, learners can experiment with lower-pressure practice, choose manageable topics, and gradually increase the difficulty as their confidence grows.</p>

      <p style="font-size:0.9rem;opacity:0.8;"><strong>Research source:</strong> AbuHussein, H. F. (2023), <em>Utilizing remote speaking tasks for tackling foreign language anxiety</em>, Cogent Education.</p>

      <h3>Celebrate Small Wins</h3>
      <p>Understand every new phrase or correct pronunciation is progress. Keep a journal of “wins” — the first time you understand a joke, or the first time you answer instantly without translating. These moments keep motivation alive.</p>

      <h3>A Simple Language Conversation Progress Tracker</h3>

      <p>Instead of measuring progress only by how many words you know, track what you can actually do during conversations.</p>

